{
  "image_name": "2cf97f694defd27acadb4c3dbf1d697e.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how innate and adaptive immunity interact against a tumor across the epithelium, lymph node, thymus, and bone marrow. On the innate side, monocytes differentiate into macrophages and dendritic cells, which release cytokines such as IL‑12, IL‑15, and IL‑18 to activate NK cells. NK cells then promote tumor cell lysis and release factors including IFNγ and HMGB1, linking early innate responses to later T‑cell activation.\n\nTumor antigens appear to be captured by dendritic cells and carried to the lymph node, where they help prime naïve T cells into T‑bet–positive TH1 cells and cytotoxic T lymphocytes (CTLs). These adaptive effector cells then return to the tumor site and contribute to additional cell killing. Cytokines including IL‑2, IL‑12, IL‑15, and IL‑27 are shown supporting this priming and effector process.\n\nThe adaptive side also includes TFH or TH17 cells producing IL‑21, as well as IFNγ and IL‑10 signaling that influence CTLs and M1 macrophages. A branch from bone marrow to thymus to naïve T cells suggests ongoing T‑cell development supported by IL‑3 and IL‑7. Overall, the image highlights a coordinated anti‑tumor immune circuit in which innate sensing, antigen presentation, and T‑cell differentiation reinforce each other.",
